  <!-- FAQPage Schema — Copilot extraction optimised -->
  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What does CISA say about PDF metadata removal?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "CISA does not publish a standalone PDF metadata removal standard, but its guidance on data minimisation, controlled unclassified information (CUI) handling, and document sanitisation — particularly through NIST SP 800-188 on de-identification and NIST SP 800-60 on information categorisation — establishes clear requirements. For documents containing CUI or personally identifiable information (PII), CISA-aligned agencies must sanitise metadata before external distribution. This means stripping the Author, Creator, Producer, Keywords, and all timestamp fields from both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does NIST SP 800-188 cover PDF metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. NIST SP 800-188 (De-Identifying Government Datasets) addresses metadata as a de-identification risk in electronic documents. Section 4 explicitly covers document metadata fields including author information, revision history, and embedded comments as potential re-identification vectors. The guidance recommends stripping these fields before publishing or sharing documents containing de-identified datasets, because metadata can re-identify individuals even when the document body has been properly de-identified."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Which PDF metadata fields does federal guidance consider high risk?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Under NIST SP 800-60 information categorisation and CISA's CUI handling requirements, the highest-risk PDF metadata fields are: Author (contains PII — a natural person's name), Manager (contains PII — a second person's name), Keywords (may contain classification codes, matter numbers, or CUI category markers), Creator (reveals software infrastructure), Producer (reveals document processing chain), and modification timestamps (reveal editing timeline). These fields are considered high risk because they can identify individuals, reveal organisational structure, expose software vulnerabilities, or disclose classified or controlled information categories."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What metadata removal method meets CISA standards for federal documents?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "CISA-aligned guidance requires complete metadata removal from both the PDF Info dictionary and the XMP stream — not just one or the other. The NSA's Media Sanitization guidance (CSEC ITSG-06) and NIST SP 800-188 both indicate that partial metadata removal is insufficient for documents containing CUI or PII. The minimum-compliant approach is: (1) run exiftool -all= document.pdf to strip both metadata stores, (2) verify with exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf to confirm the XMP stream was fully cleared, (3) for highest-assurance sanitisation, use Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function which performs a complete PDF rewrite. Printing-to-PDF does not meet federal standards as it introduces new metadata from the print driver."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is removing PDF metadata required for federal contractors?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Federal contractors handling Controlled Unclassified Information (CUI) under NIST SP 800-171 and CMMC (Cybersecurity Maturity Model Certification) have specific document sanitisation obligations. NIST SP 800-171 Control 3.13.1 requires protecting CUI during transmission and storage, which includes preventing inadvertent disclosure through document metadata. Contractors sharing PDFs with government agencies or other contractors should sanitise metadata to comply with these requirements. The specific obligation depends on the CUI category and the handling requirements in the relevant contract or agreement."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the difference between PDF sanitisation and metadata removal under federal standards?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Under federal document security standards, sanitisation is broader than metadata removal. Sanitisation covers: metadata removal (stripping Info dictionary and XMP stream), embedded object removal (hidden layers, embedded files, linked data), comment and annotation removal, revision history removal, and in some cases full document rewriting (flattening). Metadata removal is a subset of sanitisation. For documents classified or marked CUI, full sanitisation — not just metadata removal — is required. Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function performs all these steps. For documents at lower sensitivity levels, metadata removal alone is often sufficient."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <!-- HowTo Schema -->
  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "How to remove PDF metadata to meet CISA standards",
    "totalTime": "PT5M",
    "step": [
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 1, "name": "Run ExifTool strip", "text": "Run exiftool -all= document.pdf to remove all metadata from both the Info dictionary and XMP stream."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 2, "name": "Verify XMP stream", "text": "Run exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf — all fields should return empty. If any return values, the XMP stream was not fully cleared."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 3, "name": "Verify Info dictionary", "text": "Open in Acrobat Reader, go to File > Properties > Description. Author, Creator, Producer, Keywords should all be blank."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 4, "name": "Check incremental updates", "text": "Run grep -c '%%EOF' document.pdf — result should be 1. More than 1 indicates incremental updates exist."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 5, "name": "For highest assurance", "text": "Use Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function (Tools > Redact > Sanitize Document) which performs a complete file rewrite."}
    ]
  }
  </script>

