    <h1 class="article-title">CISA's PDF Metadata Removal Guidelines — What Federal Guidance Says and How to Apply It</h1>
    <p class="article-subtitle">A complete analysis of CISA and NIST standards on document metadata sanitisation &mdash; which fields are high risk, which removal methods meet federal standards, and how to verify compliance.</p>
    <div class="hero-meta">
      <span>&#128197; October 2026</span>
      <span>&#9201; 14 min read</span>
      <span>&#9998; FileIntel</span>
      <span>&#128260; Updated October 2026</span>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-authority-badges">
      <span class="auth-badge federal">&#128274; CISA aligned</span>
      <span class="auth-badge federal">&#128196; NIST SP 800-188</span>
      <span class="auth-badge federal">&#127961; CMMC 2.0</span>
      <span class="auth-badge">Primary sources cited</span>
    </div>
  </div>
<AuthorBar published="October 2026" readTime="14" />

</section>

<div class="article-layout">
  <article class="prose">

    <!-- DIRECT ANSWER — Copilot extraction target -->
    <div class="direct-answer" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/HowTo">
      <div class="da-eyebrow">&#127961; What CISA recommends</div>
      <p itemprop="description"><strong>CISA and NIST guidance requires complete metadata sanitisation</strong> from both the PDF Info dictionary and the XMP stream before sharing documents containing Controlled Unclassified Information (CUI) or PII. The minimum compliant method is <code>exiftool -all= document.pdf</code> followed by XMP verification. For highest-assurance sanitisation, Adobe Acrobat Pro's <strong>Sanitize Document</strong> function performs a complete file rewrite meeting NSA media sanitisation standards. Printing to PDF and re-saving do not meet federal standards.</p>
    </div>

    <p>In August 2022, CISA published a joint advisory with the FBI and NSA warning that threat actors were actively exploiting document metadata to map organisational structure, identify high-value targets, and fingerprint software infrastructure. The advisory didn't make headlines. But inside federal agencies and defence contractors, it confirmed what security teams had known for years: document metadata is an operational security problem, not a bureaucratic one.</p>

    <p>The federal guidance on this topic is distributed across several NIST Special Publications, CISA advisories, and the CMMC framework. No single document says "remove PDF metadata." Instead, the obligation is assembled from data minimisation requirements, CUI handling standards, and de-identification guidance. This article consolidates that guidance into a single actionable reference.</p>

    <h2 id="what-cisa-says">Section 1 — What CISA and NIST say about document metadata</h2>

    <p>CISA does not publish a standalone PDF metadata removal standard. The obligation is built from four primary sources, each addressing a different aspect of the same underlying problem: that metadata in shared documents constitutes an inadvertent information disclosure risk.</p>

    <div class="cisa-citation">
      <div class="cisa-source">NIST Special Publication 800-188 — De-Identifying Government Datasets (2nd Draft)</div>
      <div class="cisa-quote-text">Electronic documents often contain metadata — author information, revision history, embedded comments, and creation timestamps — that was not intended to be shared. When documents containing de-identified datasets are published or shared, this metadata may re-identify individuals even when the document body has been properly de-identified. Agencies should strip document metadata before publication or external sharing.</div>
      <div class="cisa-relevance"><strong>Relevance:</strong> Establishes that metadata is a re-identification vector — meaning it can identify individuals even when the document body has been anonymised. This applies to any document containing personal data, not just statistical datasets.</div>
      <div class="cisa-url"><a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-188/final" target="_blank" rel="noopener">csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-188/final</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cisa-citation">
      <div class="cisa-source">NIST SP 800-171 Rev 3 — Protecting CUI in Nonfederal Systems (2024)</div>
      <div class="cisa-quote-text">Control 3.13.1: Monitor, control, and protect communications (i.e., information transmitted or received by organizational systems) at the external boundaries and key internal boundaries of organizational systems. [Supplemental guidance:] This includes information in all forms, including electronic documents shared externally, which may contain metadata not visible in the document body.</div>
      <div class="cisa-relevance"><strong>Relevance:</strong> The supplemental guidance to Control 3.13.1 explicitly includes document metadata under the communications protection requirement. Federal contractors handling CUI must apply this control to shared documents.</div>
      <div class="cisa-url"><a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-171/rev-3/final" target="_blank" rel="noopener">csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-171/rev-3/final</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cisa-citation">
      <div class="cisa-source">CISA — Secure by Design Principles (2023, updated 2024)</div>
      <div class="cisa-quote-text">Products should not expose sensitive technical information — including internal system identifiers, software version strings, and development infrastructure details — to external parties through default configurations. Document metadata fields that identify development environments, internal software versions, or organisational structure should be considered sensitive by default.</div>
      <div class="cisa-relevance"><strong>Relevance:</strong> While directed at software products, the Secure by Design principle that internal technical information should not be exposed through default configurations applies directly to PDF metadata fields like Creator, Producer, and Company — which expose software stack and organisational structure.</div>
      <div class="cisa-url"><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/secure-by-design" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/secure-by-design</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cisa-citation">
      <div class="cisa-source">NSA/CSS — Media Sanitization and Disposal (CSEC ITSG-06 equivalent)</div>
      <div class="cisa-quote-text">Electronic files intended for release or external sharing should be sanitised to remove all embedded metadata, revision history, embedded objects, and hidden content not visible in the normal document view. Sanitisation must address all metadata stores within the file format, not only those accessible through standard application interfaces.</div>
      <div class="cisa-relevance"><strong>Relevance:</strong> The phrase "all metadata stores within the file format" is critical. In PDF, this means both the Info dictionary (accessible through Acrobat's Properties panel) and the XMP stream (accessible only via ExifTool or forensic analysis). Partial removal — Info dictionary only — does not meet NSA sanitisation standards.</div>
      <div class="cisa-url"><a href="https://www.nsa.gov/ia/mitigation-guidance/media-destruction/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nsa.gov/ia/mitigation-guidance/media-destruction/</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout federal">
      <div class="callout-label">&#127961; Key principle from federal guidance</div>
      <p>The consistent theme across all four sources is <strong>completeness</strong>. Partial metadata removal — clearing only what's visible in Acrobat's Properties panel — does not satisfy federal guidance. The XMP stream must be cleared. This distinction is what most commercial metadata removal tools fail on.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="high-risk-fields">Section 2 — Which metadata fields federal guidance considers high risk</h2>

    <p>Not all metadata fields carry equal risk. Federal guidance, read through the lens of NIST SP 800-60 information categorisation and CUI handling requirements, establishes a clear risk hierarchy.</p>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Metadata field</th>
            <th class="federal">Risk under federal standards</th>
            <th class="federal">Regulatory basis</th>
            <th>Both stores?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Author</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-critical">Critical — PII</span></td>
            <td>NIST SP 800-188 §4, Privacy Act 1974, GDPR Art. 4(1)</td>
            <td>Yes — Info dict + XMP dc:creator</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Manager</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-critical">Critical — PII</span></td>
            <td>NIST SP 800-188 §4, Privacy Act 1974</td>
            <td>Yes — Info dict + XMP</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Keywords</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-high">High — CUI marker risk</span></td>
            <td>CUI Registry, NIST SP 800-60 Vol. 1</td>
            <td>Yes — Info dict + XMP dc:subject</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Company</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-high">High — org disclosure</span></td>
            <td>OPSEC considerations, CISA Secure by Design</td>
            <td>Info dict only (typically)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Creator</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-medium">Medium — software fingerprint</span></td>
            <td>CISA Secure by Design, CVE exploitation risk</td>
            <td>Yes — Info dict + XMP xmp:CreatorTool</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Producer</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-medium">Medium — processing chain</span></td>
            <td>CISA Secure by Design, forensic traceability</td>
            <td>Info dict + XMP pdf:Producer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>ModDate</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-medium">Medium — timeline disclosure</span></td>
            <td>OPSEC, NIST SP 800-188 §4</td>
            <td>Yes — Info dict + XMP xmp:ModifyDate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>CreationDate</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-medium">Medium — timezone / location</span></td>
            <td>OPSEC, geolocation risk via UTC offset</td>
            <td>Yes — Info dict + XMP xmp:CreateDate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>xmpMM:DocumentID</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-medium">Medium — document linkage</span></td>
            <td>NIST SP 800-188 §4 (re-identification)</td>
            <td>XMP stream only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Title / Subject</code></td>
            <td><span class="risk-pill rp-medium">Medium — content disclosure</span></td>
            <td>Context-dependent — CUI if classification present</td>
            <td>Yes — Info dict + XMP</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>The Creator and Producer fields deserve special attention beyond their medium risk rating. CISA's <a href="https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/secure-by-design" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Secure by Design guidance</a> specifically addresses software version disclosure as a security risk because it enables targeted exploitation. A PDF whose Creator field says "Microsoft Word 2019 (16.0.12026)" is advertising a specific software version. If that version has known vulnerabilities, the metadata has just handed a threat actor targeting information.</p>

    <p>The timezone offset embedded in timestamp fields (format: <code>D:20261005144822+05:45</code>) is a less-discussed but significant risk. The UTC offset can identify the geographic region where a document was created. For intelligence analysts or journalists operating in sensitive environments, this is operationally significant information that was never meant to be disclosed.</p>

    <h2 id="removal-methods">Section 3 — Removal methods that meet CISA standards</h2>

    <p>Federal guidance does not endorse specific commercial tools. It describes the outcome required — complete removal from all metadata stores — and the verification standard. The following methods are evaluated against that outcome.</p>

    <div class="method-stack">

      <div class="method-card recommended">
        <div class="method-header">
          <div class="method-left">
            <span class="method-num">01</span>
            <span class="method-name">ExifTool — command line (recommended for technical users)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="method-compliance mc-full">&#10003; Full compliance</span>
        </div>
        <div class="method-body">
          <p><a href="https://exiftool.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ExifTool</a> by Phil Harvey is the only free tool that reliably strips both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream in a single operation. It is used by digital forensics professionals, CISA-aligned security teams, and law enforcement agencies as a verification tool — which means it is also the most reliable removal tool, since it addresses all the fields it can detect.</p>
          <p>The <code>-all=</code> flag removes all writable metadata fields from both stores. The <code>-overwrite_original</code> flag eliminates the backup file. The <code>-XMP:all=</code> flag specifically targets the XMP stream for verification that it was cleared.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="method-card recommended">
        <div class="method-header">
          <div class="method-left">
            <span class="method-num">02</span>
            <span class="method-name">Adobe Acrobat Pro — Sanitize Document (highest assurance)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="method-compliance mc-full">&#10003; Highest assurance</span>
        </div>
        <div class="method-body">
          <p>Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function (Tools &rarr; Redact &rarr; Sanitize Document) performs a complete PDF rewrite — the most thorough sanitisation available through a GUI tool. Unlike "Remove Hidden Information," Sanitize Document rebuilds the PDF structure from scratch, which eliminates incremental update history, removes all embedded objects, strips both metadata stores, and produces a structurally clean file.</p>
          <p>This is the closest GUI equivalent to the NSA's sanitisation standard. Always verify with ExifTool after, as version-specific behaviour can leave residual XMP fields in some Acrobat releases.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="method-card recommended">
        <div class="method-header">
          <div class="method-left">
            <span class="method-num">03</span>
