    <h2 id="verification">Section 4 — How to verify compliance</h2>

    <p>Verification is the step most organisations skip. Running a sanitisation tool and assuming it worked is not sufficient for federal compliance. NSA media sanitisation guidance explicitly requires verification that the sanitisation achieved the intended outcome.</p>

    <p>The following verification sequence covers every metadata layer. Mark each step complete as you work through it:</p>

    <div class="verify-steps" id="verify-steps">
      <div class="verify-step" onclick="toggleVerify(this)">
        <div class="vs-num">1</div>
        <div class="vs-content">
          <div class="vs-title">Verify Author and Manager fields — Info dictionary</div>
          <div class="vs-cmd">exiftool -Author -Manager document.pdf</div>
          <div class="vs-result">Both lines must return empty. Any name returned indicates incomplete sanitisation.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="verify-step" onclick="toggleVerify(this)">
        <div class="vs-num">2</div>
        <div class="vs-content">
          <div class="vs-title">Verify XMP stream is fully cleared</div>
          <div class="vs-cmd">exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</div>
          <div class="vs-result">No output is the correct result. Any XMP field returned means the XMP stream was not cleared — this is the most common failure point.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="verify-step" onclick="toggleVerify(this)">
        <div class="vs-num">3</div>
        <div class="vs-content">
          <div class="vs-title">Verify Keywords — potential CUI marker exposure</div>
          <div class="vs-cmd">exiftool -Keywords -XMP:Subject document.pdf</div>
          <div class="vs-result">Both must be empty. Keywords may contain DMS classification codes or CUI category markers even after apparent sanitisation.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="verify-step" onclick="toggleVerify(this)">
        <div class="vs-num">4</div>
        <div class="vs-content">
          <div class="vs-title">Verify Creator and Producer — software fingerprint</div>
          <div class="vs-cmd">exiftool -Creator -Producer -XMP:CreatorTool document.pdf</div>
          <div class="vs-result">All three must be empty. XMP:CreatorTool is often overlooked — it persists in the XMP stream after the Info dictionary Creator is cleared.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="verify-step" onclick="toggleVerify(this)">
        <div class="vs-num">5</div>
        <div class="vs-content">
          <div class="vs-title">Verify timestamps — including timezone offset</div>
          <div class="vs-cmd">exiftool -CreateDate -ModifyDate -XMP:CreateDate -XMP:ModifyDate -MetadataDate document.pdf</div>
          <div class="vs-result">All five fields must be empty. MetadataDate (XMP) is a separate field that records when the metadata itself was last written — it persists after incomplete sanitisation.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="verify-step" onclick="toggleVerify(this)">
        <div class="vs-num">6</div>
        <div class="vs-content">
          <div class="vs-title">Verify no incremental updates — structural check</div>
          <div class="vs-cmd">grep -c "%%EOF" document.pdf</div>
          <div class="vs-result">Must return 1. Any higher number indicates incremental updates exist in the file structure — edit history that survives metadata stripping and requires a full PDF rewrite to eliminate.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="verify-step" onclick="toggleVerify(this)">
        <div class="vs-num">7</div>
        <div class="vs-content">
          <div class="vs-title">Verify in Acrobat Reader — human-readable confirmation</div>
          <div class="vs-cmd">File → Properties → Description tab</div>
          <div class="vs-result">All visible fields should be blank. This is the view recipients see. Note: Acrobat Properties does NOT show the XMP stream — which is why ExifTool verification is required in addition to this step.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout green">
      <div class="callout-label">&#10003; Documentation requirement for federal contractors</div>
      <p>For documents submitted to federal agencies under CUI handling requirements, maintain a sanitisation log including: the document filename, the sanitisation tool and version used, the date of sanitisation, the operator who performed it, and the ExifTool verification output confirming all fields were cleared. This log supports audit requirements under CMMC 2.0 Level 2 practice CA.L2-3.12.4 (assess security controls) and MP.L2-3.8.3 (sanitize or destroy information system media).</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="limitations">Section 5 — Limitations CISA guidance acknowledges</h2>

    <p>Federal guidance is explicit about what metadata sanitisation does not address. Understanding these limitations is essential for security professionals who need to know where the boundary of their sanitisation procedure sits.</p>

    <p><strong>Scanned documents.</strong> A PDF created by scanning a physical document is an image-based PDF. It has minimal metadata but the image itself may contain printer tracking dots — microscopic yellow dots embedded by colour laser printers that encode the printer's serial number and print date. ExifTool does not detect or remove these. CISA's printer security guidance (Alert TA18-106A) addresses this separately.</p>

    <p><strong>Embedded objects and linked data.</strong> Metadata sanitisation removes the Info dictionary and XMP stream. It does not remove embedded files, linked data sources, OLE objects, or macros that may carry separate metadata. Acrobat's Sanitize Document function addresses some of these; a full sanitisation for classified documents requires more comprehensive review.</p>

    <p><strong>Content-derived re-identification.</strong> NIST SP 800-188 explicitly notes that removing metadata does not prevent re-identification through document content itself — writing style, specific terminology, unique formatting choices, or rare data combinations within the body text can identify the author even with all metadata stripped. Metadata sanitisation is a necessary but not sufficient control for full anonymisation.</p>

    <p><strong>Digital signature invalidation.</strong> Metadata sanitisation of a digitally signed document will invalidate the digital signature, because the signature covers the document content including the metadata stores. Federal agencies should sanitise metadata before applying digital signatures, not after. If a pre-signed document requires sanitisation, a new signature must be applied after sanitisation.</p>

    <p><strong>Forensic residue in slack space.</strong> PDF file structure can leave residual data in areas of the file not covered by the cross-reference table. This is addressable only by a complete file rewrite (QPDF linearisation or Acrobat Sanitize Document). ExifTool alone does not perform a file rewrite and does not address forensic residue in file slack space.</p>

    <div class="callout amber">
      <div class="callout-label">&#9888; The partial clean failure mode</div>
      <p>The most common compliance failure in federal document sanitisation is tools that clear the Info dictionary but leave the XMP stream intact. From the Acrobat Properties panel, the document appears fully sanitised — all fields blank. Running ExifTool reveals the complete original metadata in the XMP namespace. For any document containing CUI or PII, ExifTool verification of the XMP stream is not optional.</p>
    </div>

