    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 class="faq-title">Security professionals ask</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What does CISA say about PDF metadata removal?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">CISA does not publish a standalone PDF metadata removal standard. The obligation comes from NIST SP 800-188 (de-identification), NIST SP 800-171 Control 3.13.1 (CUI communications protection), and NSA media sanitisation guidance. Taken together, these require complete metadata removal from both the PDF Info dictionary and the XMP stream before sharing documents containing CUI or PII. Partial removal — Info dictionary only — does not satisfy the NSA standard of removing metadata from "all metadata stores within the file format."</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Does NIST SP 800-188 cover PDF metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Yes. Section 4 of NIST SP 800-188 addresses document metadata — including author information, revision history, and embedded comments — as re-identification vectors. The guidance recommends stripping these fields before publishing or sharing documents containing de-identified datasets, because metadata can re-identify individuals even when the document body has been properly de-identified. The full publication is at csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-188/final.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Which PDF metadata fields does federal guidance consider high risk?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Under NIST SP 800-60 and CISA CUI handling requirements, the critical-risk fields are Author and Manager (both contain PII — natural persons' names). High-risk fields include Keywords (may contain CUI category markers or classification codes from document management systems) and Company (organisational disclosure). Medium-risk fields include Creator and Producer (software fingerprint enabling CVE targeting), and timestamp fields (contain timezone offsets that may reveal geographic location).</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What PDF metadata removal method meets CISA standards for federal documents?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">The minimum-compliant approach is: (1) exiftool -all= document.pdf to strip both metadata stores, (2) verify with exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf to confirm the XMP stream is fully cleared. For highest-assurance sanitisation meeting NSA media sanitisation standards, use Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function (Tools > Redact > Sanitize Document) which performs a complete PDF rewrite. Always follow Acrobat sanitisation with ExifTool XMP verification, as version-specific behaviour can leave residual fields. Printing to PDF and Acrobat's "Remove Hidden Information" function do not meet federal standards.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Is removing PDF metadata required for federal contractors?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Federal contractors handling CUI under NIST SP 800-171 and CMMC 2.0 have document sanitisation obligations under Control 3.13.1 (communications protection) and MP.L2-3.8.3 (media sanitisation). The specific obligation depends on the CUI category and handling requirements in the relevant contract. At a minimum, documents containing CUI or PII shared externally should have Author, Manager, Keywords, and all timestamp fields stripped from both the Info dictionary and XMP stream. Maintain a sanitisation log for audit purposes under CMMC 2.0 Level 2 CA.L2-3.12.4.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What is the difference between PDF sanitisation and metadata removal under federal standards?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Sanitisation is broader than metadata removal. Under NSA media sanitisation guidance and NIST standards, full document sanitisation covers: metadata removal (Info dictionary + XMP stream), embedded object removal, hidden layer removal, comment and annotation removal, revision history elimination, and complete file rewriting. Metadata removal alone addresses only the first item. For documents at lower sensitivity levels (uncontrolled unclassified information shared with cleared counterparts), metadata removal is often sufficient. For documents containing CUI or approaching classified categories, full sanitisation is required.</div></div>
      </div>

